WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:16.125
FLOWERS: 270 started
in about 1970.

00:00:16.125 --> 00:00:17.500
At that time, our
department head

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.630
was in charge of the
first design course--

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:20.880
Herb Richardson.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.734
And we gave the students
a creativity kit.

00:00:23.734 --> 00:00:25.400
And I was a graduate
student at the time

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:27.240
and worked closely
with the students

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.880
and learned that, for
their first design project,

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.770
it was really hard for
them to decide what to do.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:34.770
So the next year, we
decided that we'd give them

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.770
a kit of materials but give
them a very specific thing

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:39.020
to try to accomplish.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:40.800
And they got into
it immediately,

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.080
and that sort of got
the ball rolling.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.800
And 270 began with a
very simple contest

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.790
that involved making
the machine go down

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:51.520
a hill in three minutes.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.760
Back then, we had about
30 students in the class,

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:56.560
and maybe another
15 or 20 people

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.070
came to watch the contest.

00:00:58.070 --> 00:00:59.980
And it happened in
one of our classrooms.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:02.530
It was a very modest beginning.

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:06.370
Actually, the genesis of the
design-and-build part of 270

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:09.460
came from noticing that
most kids had never

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:13.150
really designed and built and
seen Mother Nature evaluate

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:13.960
something.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.720
So we thought that the
design process would best

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:19.900
be illustrated by getting
them in there and doing it.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.150
The thing that's kind
of different about 270

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:26.210
is that it was born
a little premature.

00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:29.380
We started doing it before
other folks got into it.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.830
But it grew up really strong.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:36.430
And it grew up with
a great attitude.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:40.570
The students here make
the contest what it is,

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:44.200
and they go at it with an
enthusiasm and a commitment

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.150
and a sense of
helping one another

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.800
that's wonderfully unique.

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.030
I think the thing that makes
the 270 experience quite unique

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:55.430
is that, for most
of the students,

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:59.720
they've never done a
really public demonstration

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:02.540
of their ability to conceive
something and build it.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.370
And they kind of give
birth to something.

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:08.389
And they watch it as it
goes out and does its task.

00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:11.570
And that's done in a very
celebratory environment.

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:13.790
The students all know
that we'll still love them

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:15.440
if the machine doesn't work.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.400
But it's done under a
great deal of pressure.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.950
So the mix of pressure
and forgiveness,

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:26.147
and the process of really doing
something, is a great mix.

00:02:26.147 --> 00:02:27.980
One of the things that's
important about 270

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.610
is that it comes at the end of
a great deal of expectation.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:33.560
The students have
seen it on television.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:34.800
They know about it.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.560
They think about it in advance.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:38.300
And they get into
it, and it ends up

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:42.040
being a really exciting
thing to do, for some reason

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:43.470
that I don't really understand.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:46.800
But building something and
launching it out into the world

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.040
and having it do
is really exciting.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.420
So they think about it a lot.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:53.489
They work on it really hard.

00:02:53.489 --> 00:02:55.530
And after it's over,
there's a tremendous feeling

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.000
of satisfaction and relief.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.210
And that's an important
event in one's life.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.210
One of the ways to think
about why 270 is important

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.460
is to look at Thomas Massie.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:10.110
Thomas came to MIT because when
he was in the seventh grade,

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:13.020
he saw the 270 contest
on television and said,

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.690
I want to go win that.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:18.210
And he came to MIT
as he was a senior

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.440
in electrical engineering.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:23.880
He took the course
and won the contest.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:27.370
Obviously, lots of kids
think about doing that.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:28.386
It's a big deal.

00:03:28.386 --> 00:03:29.760
One of the things
that's happened

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.590
is that the culture
of the course

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:34.620
has evolved in a way
that helping one another

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.930
is one of the core ideas.

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:41.670
That came from the students, and
we have allowed it to flourish,

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:44.250
and it's now part of
the core tradition.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:45.720
They learn from
one another a lot

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.550
more than they learn from us.

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:50.310
And I really like
seeing that happen.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.550
You know, the thing that's
really important about the 270

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.940
deal is that it's a
shared commitment.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:01.020
The students hear from
the staff and the faculty

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:05.004
that we're going to work hard
to make this thing work well.

00:04:05.004 --> 00:04:06.420
We're going to put
a lot of energy

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.930
into defining the contests
so that it's interesting.

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:13.080
And we're going to work hard
to support you and help you.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:14.820
And the students
respond to that.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.890
And they work hard, and
they put a lot into it.

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:21.360
And that deal, where
everyone is agreeing

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.400
that this is
something important,

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:26.190
is the key ingredient
that makes it work.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:28.800
I think one of the really
nice features of the course

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.440
is that it gives the
students an opportunity

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.090
to remodel themselves.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:35.910
They discover that
they can, in fact,

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:41.040
take a chaotic kit of materials
and use their creativity

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.600
and make something
wonderful happen.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.350
And if you trust
your own creativity,

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:48.960
you're very likely to have
a more satisfying life

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:50.430
than otherwise.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:53.790
I think, probably, the single
most important ingredient

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:55.770
of a good contest
is to make sure

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.950
that it has been
ambivalence-scaled right,

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:01.860
so that even the staff
or the people that

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:05.170
conceived the contest have no
idea what's the best solution.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:08.239
So if you balance
things properly, then

00:05:08.239 --> 00:05:10.030
among the people that
are talking about it,

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:12.540
there's a heavy argument
about which way to go.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:15.790
That drives diverse solutions.

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:18.990
In fact, one of the contests
that I designed some years ago,

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:20.760
called The Potentially
Great Race,

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:25.230
was terrible because all
the solutions looked alike.

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:26.520
It was optimizable.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:27.510
It was predictable.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:30.550
And I just didn't
see that in advance.

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:33.340
One of my favorite 270
stories centers around a slide

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:36.580
that I have of a student
looking at her machine

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:39.790
as it goes over the top
of a sand-covered hill.

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:43.150
And it's important because she
had come to my office crying.

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:44.590
I hate this course.

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:45.580
This is terrible.

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:46.950
I've never built anything.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.570
I'm not going to make a
good grade, et cetera.

00:05:49.570 --> 00:05:52.180
And she hung in there,
did it, her machine

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:53.840
did what she wanted it to do.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.020
And she ended up
becoming a designer.

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:57.640
I like that story.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:00.710
The 270 contest evolved
to where it is today.

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:03.370
When we first started,
it was a simple machine

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:05.770
doing a single thing
while people watched.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:07.420
And it was quite slow.

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:10.540
After a while, we had machines,
side by side, doing things,

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:12.160
so you could see
some competition.

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.380
And then, we got
interested in, what

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:17.080
happens if the machines
actually collide in the middle

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:19.330
and compete for territory?

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:22.964
And from that, we evolved
robotically-controlled things,

00:06:22.964 --> 00:06:24.880
so that they were not
automatic, that students

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:27.280
were controlling them while
they were doing things.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:31.240
And that has become a much more
interactive, interesting thing

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:32.440
to watch.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:34.690
Actually, I have two favorites.

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:36.580
One was called
The Harvest, which

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:40.450
involved a wavy table
and 300 ping-pong balls.

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:43.660
And it was really a
wonderfully dramatic contest

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:46.900
because of the diverse ways
the students approached it.

00:06:46.900 --> 00:06:50.890
The other was called The Summit
Meeting, which was a mountain

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:53.109
and involved raising
a ping-pong ball

00:06:53.109 --> 00:06:54.650
high over the summit
of the mountain.

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:57.430
And the students built
wonderful, tall towers

00:06:57.430 --> 00:06:59.020
and diverse machines.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:01.430
And they both just worked well.

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:03.100
We all want to be happy.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:05.050
Wise people have
pointed out that you

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:07.750
can't make people happy by
taking away the things that

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:09.580
make them unhappy.

00:07:09.580 --> 00:07:11.740
In this course,
many students learn

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:15.730
that they can become happy
through the satisfaction

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:17.210
of being creative.

00:07:17.210 --> 00:07:19.540
And that's really
a wonderful lesson.

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:24.390
Mother Nature applies all of
her rules all of the time.

